window.TOO_LONG = {"model": {"title": "Open-source wedding/event RSVP + seating system — Architecture Proposal", "file": "pasted-content-id-36de-i-want-silly-scott.md", "intro": [], "sections": [{"id": "context", "title": "Context", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Greenfield repo (`/Users/galhavshush/Projects/rsvp-system`, empty). Self-hosted modular monolith: FastAPI + PostgreSQL + React/Vite/TS/Tailwind, Hebrew-first RTL, WhatsApp deferred behind a provider interface (CSV test mode). This document answers the 30 requested points; each decision = **Rec / Why / Alt / Tradeoff**. Nothing is implemented until you approve."}, {"type": "hr"}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "1-domain-model", "title": "1. Domain model", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Aggregates: **Event** → owns **Group**, **Invitation** (→ **Member**, **Contact**), **Table**, **Snapshot**, **SeatingConstraint**, **MessageTemplate**, **MessageBatch**. **User** is global (admins). Everything event-scoped carries `event_id`."}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Invitation = RSVP unit **and** default Seating Party (`seats_needed = attendee_count` when status=coming).", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Seating assignment is a row linking Invitation → Table; layout lives on Table. Never mixed.", "ordered": false}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "2-database-schema-postgresql-alembic", "title": "2. Database schema (PostgreSQL, Alembic)", "blocks": [{"type": "code", "lang": "", "text": "user(id, email unique, password_hash, role[owner|admin], created_at, disabled)\nevent(id, name, type, date, time, venue, address, waze_url, maps_url, hosts, rsvp_deadline,\n      default_language, notes, created_at)\nguest_group(id, event_id, name, color; unique(event_id,name))\ninvitation(id, event_id, display_name, group_id?, rsvp_token unique, rsvp_status[no_response|coming|not_coming|maybe],\n      attendee_count int?, rsvp_updated_at?, rsvp_source[guest_web|admin_manual|whatsapp]?, rsvp_note, notes,\n      include_maybe_in_seating bool default false, message_state[not_prepared|prepared|exported])\ninvitation_member(id, invitation_id, name, position)\ninvitation_contact(id, invitation_id, member_id?, phone_raw, phone_e164?, phone_valid, position)\nseating_table(id, event_id, name, capacity, x, y, shape[round|rect], width, height, zone?, notes,\n      position_locked, assignments_locked)\nseat_assignment(invitation_id PK/unique, table_id, locked bool)      -- one row per seated invitation\nseating_constraint(id, event_id, type[together|apart], invitation_a, invitation_b)\nseating_snapshot(id, event_id, label, kind[before_auto|auto|manual|final], created_at, data jsonb)\nmessage_template(id, event_id, name, body, language)\nmessage_batch(id, event_id, provider, created_at, recipient_count)   -- audit of exports"}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "CHECK `attendee_count >= 0`; partial index on `phone_e164`; `seat_assignment.invitation_id` unique ⇒ a party sits at one table. Snapshots store `{tables:[{id,x,y,...}], assignments:[{inv,table,locked}]}` as JSONB (small, restore = transactional replace). `ON DELETE CASCADE` from event ⇒ deleting an event/invitation really deletes personal data."}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "3-authentication", "title": "3. Authentication", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "**Rec:** server-side sessions in a `session` table (random 256-bit id, hash stored), `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` cookie; argon2id via `argon2-cffi`; CSRF via double-submit header for mutating requests (SameSite=Lax + custom `X-Requested-With` check is sufficient for same-origin deployment). `/api/auth/setup` works only while `user` table is empty → creates Owner. Owner creates Admins. FastAPI dependencies `current_admin`, `require_owner`. **Alt:** JWT in cookie (can't revoke, no benefit); fastapi-users (heavy, pulls in OAuth/etc. we explicitly don't want). **Tradeoff:** one extra DB lookup per request — irrelevant at this scale. Login rate limit."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "4-event-model", "title": "4. Event model", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Straight table above; `type` is a free string/enum (`wedding` default) — no wedding-only columns. Single active event in UI via an event switcher (data model supports many)."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "5-invitation-member-contact", "title": "5. Invitation / member / contact", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Normalized as specified. Contact optionally links to a member. Display name independent of members. Spreadsheet's `person_N/phone_N` exist only in import/export adapters."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "6-rsvp-state-model", "title": "6. RSVP state model", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Single set of columns on `invitation`. One service function `rsvp_service.set_rsvp(invitation, status, count, note, source)` used by **both** public endpoint and admin endpoint ⇒ one system. Rules: `coming` ⇒ count ≥ 1 (default = member count or 1); other statuses ⇒ count null (or 0 for not_coming). Sets `rsvp_updated_at`, `rsvp_source`. No history table (MVP). Changing status away from `coming` doesn't delete the seat assignment automatically; it raises a seating warning (\"seated but not coming\") — admin decides."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "7-secure-rsvp-tokens", "title": "7. Secure RSVP tokens", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "`secrets.token_urlsafe(24)` (192 bits) stored in `invitation.rsvp_token` (unique index). Plain storage is acceptable: it is an unguessable bearer link that admins must re-display/export; hashing would prevent re-generating CSVs. Admin can \"regenerate token\" (invalidates old link). Public endpoints: `GET/PUT /api/public/rsvp/{token}`; return only display name, event name/date/venue/links, current RSVP, member first names optional (off by default). Rate limit by IP (slowapi, in-memory; Redis only if multi-worker later) + uniform 404 for bad tokens. `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, `noindex`, no third-party scripts."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "8-import-architecture", "title": "8. Import architecture", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Pipeline of pure functions, server-side, stateless-in-DB until commit: `upload → parse (openpyxl / csv, size+row caps, no macros/formulas evaluated) → ParsedSheet(headers, rows) → detect_mapping → apply mapping → normalize (phones, trim) → validate + dedupe → ImportPreview → commit`."}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Upload returns `import_id`; parsed sheet kept in a temp row/file (TTL) so mapping can be re-applied without re-upload. Preview endpoint takes `{import_id, mapping, conflict_actions}`. Commit is one transaction.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Output of normalization: `list[InvitationDraft(name, members[], contacts[], group)]` — the same DTO the manual create endpoint uses. **Alt:** do everything client-side (SheetJS) — duplicates logic in TS, can't detect DB duplicates. Rejected.", "ordered": false}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "9-column-mapping", "title": "9. Column mapping", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Mapping = `{column_index → target}`, target ∈ `invitation_name | group | person[n] | phone[n] | note | ignore`. Detector: normalized header (lowercase, strip, collapse spaces) matched against synonym dictionary (he/en) plus regex `^(שם|person|name)\\s*(\\d+)$` / `^(טלפון|phone|mobile|נייד)\\s*(\\d+)$`. Unmapped → \"ignore\", user can fix in UI. Person N / Phone N pair by N. Dictionary in one data file (`imports/headers.py`) so new languages = data change."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "10-phone-normalization", "title": "10. Phone normalization", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "`phonenumbers` (Python port of libphonenumber). `normalize(raw, default_region=settings.DEFAULT_REGION=\"IL\")` → E.164 or invalid. Handles `050-…`, `972…`, `+972…`. Default region is an env/event setting ⇒ not Israel-only. Original kept in `phone_raw`. Dup detection by `phone_e164`."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "11-messaging-provider-architecture", "title": "11. Messaging-provider architecture", "blocks": [{"type": "code", "lang": "python", "text": "class MessagingProvider(Protocol):\n    def prepare_message(self, inv, contact, template, ctx) -> PreparedMessage\n    def send_message(self, msgs: list[PreparedMessage]) -> SendResult   # test: produces CSV artifact\n    def process_webhook(self, headers, body) -> list[StatusUpdate]      # test: NotImplemented\n    def get_delivery_status(self, msg_id) -> Status"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Rendering (`render_template`, `build_prepared_messages`) is **provider-independent** and lives in `messaging/service.py`; providers only transport. Registry picks provider by `MESSAGING_PROVIDER=test_csv`. Honest note: `prepare_message` is better as shared core logic than a provider method; I keep it on the interface only as a hook (WhatsApp needs template-parameter mapping). Rec: **`render` in core; provider gets `to_transport_payload(PreparedMessage)`**. Shipping with just the 4-method Protocol you listed minus anything unused would be simplest — flag if you want exactly your 4."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "12-testcsvmessagingprovider", "title": "12. TestCsvMessagingProvider", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "`send_message(msgs)` writes UTF-8-**with-BOM** CSV (so Excel renders Hebrew) columns: Invitation Name, Recipient Name, Phone Number, RSVP Link, Message. Returns bytes streamed as download; records `message_batch`; sets invitation `message_state=exported` (never \"sent\"). Per-recipient exclusion is request-level (list of contact ids), not stored."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "13-preparedmessage", "title": "13. PreparedMessage", "blocks": [{"type": "code", "lang": "python", "text": "@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass PreparedMessage:\n    invitation_id; invitation_name; recipient_name; contact_id\n    phone_number (raw); normalized_phone_number; rsvp_url; rendered_message; language"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Template variables: `{recipient_name} {invitation_name} {rsvp_url} {event_name} {event_date} {venue}`; rendered with a restricted `str.format_map`-style substitution (no Jinja ⇒ no template injection). Contacts with invalid phone are listed as \"excluded: invalid phone\" in preview."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "14-future-whatsappcloudmessagingprovider", "title": "14. Future WhatsAppCloudMessagingProvider", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Same Protocol. Adds: template-name + param mapping, HTTP client (httpx) to Graph API, `POST /api/webhooks/whatsapp` (GET verify challenge + `X-Hub-Signature-256` HMAC check with app secret), `message_delivery(message_id, contact_id, status, ts)` table, retry via a small worker (arq/RQ) — **added only then**. Interactive reply buttons → `rsvp_service.set_rsvp(source='whatsapp')`. Inbound phone → invitation via `phone_e164` match. Zero changes to RSVP/invitation code."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "15-react-structure", "title": "15. React structure", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Vite + React 18 + TS + React Router + TanStack Query + Tailwind, as you listed: `src/{features/{auth,events,invitations,import,rsvp,messaging,seating},components,api,hooks,i18n,layouts,types,utils}`. Two route trees/layouts: `AdminLayout` (auth-guarded) and `GuestLayout` (tiny, lazy-loaded chunk so RSVP page stays fast). Local UI state via `useState/useReducer`; seating canvas local state (drag in progress) separate from server state with optimistic mutations. UI kit: Tailwind + **Radix UI primitives** (dialogs, dropdowns, popovers; direction-aware) with shadcn/ui-style copy-in components. Table: TanStack Table."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "16-rest-api-structure-api", "title": "16. REST API structure (`/api`)", "blocks": [{"type": "code", "lang": "", "text": "auth:        POST setup | login | logout ; GET me ; POST/GET users (owner)\nevents:      CRUD /events\ngroups:      CRUD /events/{e}/groups\ninvitations: CRUD /events/{e}/invitations (+filters/sort/search/pagination) ; PUT /invitations/{id}/rsvp ;\n             POST /invitations/{id}/regenerate-token ; nested members/contacts CRUD ; GET /events/{e}/invitations/export\nimports:     POST /events/{e}/imports (upload) ; POST /imports/{id}/preview ; POST /imports/{id}/commit\nmessaging:   GET/PUT templates ; POST /events/{e}/messages/preview ; POST /events/{e}/messages/export (CSV)\ndashboard:   GET /events/{e}/stats\ntables:      CRUD /events/{e}/tables ; POST /tables/{id}/duplicate\nseating:     GET /events/{e}/seating (tables+assignments+warnings) ; PUT/DELETE /seating/assignments ;\n             POST /seating/auto ; snapshots: GET/POST/POST restore ; constraints CRUD ; GET export?format=by_table|alpha\npublic:      GET/PUT /public/rsvp/{token}"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Backend layout per domain: `app/<domain>/{router.py, service.py, models.py, schemas.py}` + `app/core/{db,config,security}`; routers thin, logic in services; pure algorithms (phone, mapping, optimizer, capacity) in dependency-free modules → unit-testable without DB."}, {"type": "p", "text": "**Typed client:** generate TS types from FastAPI OpenAPI with **`openapi-typescript` + `openapi-fetch`** (tiny, no codegen runtime). Alt: orval (generates TanStack Query hooks; more magic, bigger output), hey-api. Tradeoff: I hand-write thin query hooks, but types never drift; `npm run gen:api` + CI check that generated file is current."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "1719-seating-editor-libraries-tablevenue-model", "title": "17–19. Seating editor, libraries, table/venue model", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "**Rec:** two layers."}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "**Canvas = SVG (or absolutely-positioned divs in a transformed container) in React**, with pan/zoom via a small transform (`react-zoom-pan-pinch` or hand-rolled ~40 lines). Table dragging via **`@dnd-kit/core`** pointer sensors (touch + mouse + keyboard, no HTML5-DnD flakiness), snapping to optional grid, collision/overlap checked in my own simple AABB function. Canvas uses `direction: ltr` on its container and logical X/Y in px ⇒ never mirrored by RTL.", "ordered": true, "n": 1}, {"text": "**Invitation drag** from the Unassigned panel / between tables uses the **same dnd-kit `DndContext`**: tables are droppables, invitation chips are draggables. One library for both ⇒ one mental model. Panel and canvas share a context; auto-scroll handled by dnd-kit.", "ordered": true, "n": 2}]}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Selection: click/shift-click on tables (simple state); no marquee in MVP.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "**Alternatives:** *react-konva* (canvas; great perf for hundreds of nodes, but a11y poor, DnD from DOM panel → canvas needs custom hit-testing, text/Hebrew rendering in canvas is meh); *react-flow/xyflow* (graph editor, brings zoom/pan/drag for free but is node/edge-centric and awkward for dropping DOM items onto nodes + licensing/branding nudge); *react-dnd* (HTML5 backend poor on touch); *tldraw* (too heavy, licence restrictions). dnd-kit + SVG is best for ≤ ~60 tables, which is realistic for weddings.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "RTL: dnd-kit deltas are screen-space, so no mirroring bug; only the *panel/sidebar position* flips with `dir`, canvas stays LTR; `dir=\"auto\"` on user text. Test in both directions.", "ordered": false}]}, {"type": "p", "text": "**Table & layout:** `seating_table` holds layout (x,y,shape,w,h,zone); `seat_assignment` holds assignment. Moving a table updates only x/y. Future items (stage, dance floor, background image) = a future `venue_object` table + `event.floorplan_image`; the canvas just renders more layers. Not built now."}, {"type": "p", "text": "**Persistence:** drag-end `PATCH` of table position (debounced for keyboard nudge), optimistic update."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "20-seating-party", "title": "20. Seating Party", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Party = Invitation. `seats_needed = attendee_count` if coming; `maybe` only when `include_maybe_in_seating`; others excluded. `unassigned` panel lists eligible parties not in `seat_assignment`, with RSVP badge, group color, count. Splitting later = new `seating_party` table; the optimizer already works over \"parties\" with `size`, so adding that is localized. Not built now."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "2123-automatic-seating", "title": "21–23. Automatic seating", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "**Rec: OR-Tools CP-SAT**, deterministic (`random_seed=0`, `num_workers=1`, fixed time limit e.g. 10 s; inputs sorted by id)."}, {"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Vars: `x[p,t]` ∈ {0,1}. Each party → ≤1 table.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "**Hard:** Σ size·x ≤ capacity − locked_occupancy per table; locked assignments & locked tables fixed (parties on locked tables are removed from the problem, their occupancy is subtracted); `together` pairs equal tables; `apart` pairs not equal; parties never split.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "**Soft (maximize score, weights in one `SeatingWeights` dataclass in `seating/config.py`, overridable by env):** same-group pairs co-seated (+), group isolation penalty (party alone of its group at a table when group has ≥2 parties), underfill penalty (table occupancy < threshold fraction), unassigned penalty (very large, so seating everyone is preferred but infeasible instances still return a partial result), mild preference to keep parties at their *current* table when mode=reoptimize (stability).", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Pairwise same-group terms: linearized per (group, table) with `n[g,t]` counts → cheaper than pair vars: reward `min`-style via concave piecewise terms, or simply `y[g,t]` \"group present at table\" minimized (fewer tables per group). I'll use \"minimize number of tables per group\" + isolation — simple and explainable.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Modes: `unassigned_only` (all existing assignments fixed) and `reoptimize_unlocked`.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Output: assignments + **explanation** (per-party reasons, list of unplaced parties and why) — no black box. **Locking:** `seat_assignment.locked` (per assignment) + `table.assignments_locked` (all on that table) + `table.position_locked` (layout only). Invitation-level lock = assignment lock. **Capacity over-allocation recommendation:** *Yes, allow manual overflow with a strong red warning (e.g. `11/10`), never automatic.* Why: real venues add chairs; blocking drops would force workarounds. Cost: a soft invariant instead of a DB constraint, so warnings are computed server-side and shown live. Optimizer treats capacity as hard. **Conflicts** (computed by `seating/conflicts.py`, pure function, returned with each `GET /seating` and mutation): over_capacity, locked_conflict (move blocked by lock → UI asks to unlock), party_split (n/a until splitting), apart_violation, together_violation, unassigned_confirmed, seated_but_not_coming, maybe_seated. Auto Seat endpoint: snapshot \"Before Auto Seat\" → run solver → write assignments → return diff; the editor remains fully interactive.", "ordered": false}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "24-snapshots", "title": "24. Snapshots", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "JSONB of layout + assignments (+locks). Auto-created before Auto Seat; manual \"Save snapshot\"; restore = auto-snapshot current (\"Before restore\") then replace tables/assignments in one transaction (restoring tables by id; deleted tables are recreated). Undo/redo: deferred — snapshots cover the need."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "25-rtl-i18n", "title": "25. RTL / i18n", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "**react-i18next** + JSON namespaces `he.json`/`en.json` (Hebrew default, lazy-loaded), ESLint `i18next/no-literal-string` to enforce zero hardcoded strings. `<html lang dir>` updated on language change; Tailwind **logical utilities** (`ms-*`, `ps-*`, `text-start`, `start-0`) — never `ml/left`. Radix `DirectionProvider`. Numbers/dates via `Intl` with locale (Hebrew locale; Gregorian default, optional Hebrew calendar later). Bidi: `dir=\"auto\"` or `<bdi>` for names/phones (phones forced `dir=\"ltr\"`). Icons that imply direction (chevrons, back) flip; seating canvas explicitly `dir=ltr`. Font: Heebo/Assistant (self-hosted via fontsource — no Google CDN, privacy). Backend messages are error *codes* translated client-side; message templates carry a `language`. Playwright tests run in both languages."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "26-docker-self-hosting", "title": "26. Docker / self-hosting", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "`docker compose up`: services `db` (postgres:16, named volume `pgdata`), `api` (python:3.12-slim, runs `alembic upgrade head` on start, uvicorn), `web` (multi-stage build → static files served by **Caddy**, which also reverse-proxies `/api` to `api` → same origin, so cookies/CSRF trivial; automatic HTTPS in prod via `DOMAIN` env). Dev override: `docker-compose.dev.yml` with Vite HMR + uvicorn `--reload`. `.env.example`; `SECRET_KEY` generated-instructions; `make seed` / `python -m app.seed` for demo. Docs: backup `pg_dump`, restore `psql`, upgrade steps. **Alt:** nginx (more config), FastAPI serving static (couples build, fine but less standard). One worker process initially ⇒ in-memory rate limiter acceptable (documented ceiling)."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "27-security-privacy", "title": "27. Security / privacy", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "Argon2id, session table + revocation, CSRF header, secure cookies, login throttling, CORS closed (same-origin), Pydantic validation everywhere, upload cap (e.g. 5 MB / 5k rows), openpyxl `read_only`, `data_only=True`, never execute formulas; CSV injection defense on export (prefix `'` to cells starting `= + - @`; keeps Excel-safe). Tokens 192-bit; public API minimal; security headers via Caddy; secrets only via env; `.gitignore` for `.env`; logs omit phones/tokens. Delete event/invitation = hard delete cascade. `PRIVACY.md`: what goes to Meta later (recipient phone, rendered message text/template params, RSVP link; inbound message content/phone from webhook) and that nothing leaves the server today; no analytics/fonts/CDNs on RSVP page. Webhook later: HMAC verification, verify-token handshake."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "28-testing", "title": "28. Testing", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Backend **pytest** + real Postgres (testcontainers or compose service): auth/session, phone normalization (table-driven), header detection, import pipeline + duplicates, invitation/member/contact create, token entropy/uniqueness & 404 behavior, RSVP update (public + admin share same state), message rendering, CSV output (BOM, one row per contact, CSV-injection), capacity logic, locks, optimizer constraints (hard constraints hold, locked untouched, deterministic: same input ⇒ same output, group clustering), snapshot restore round-trip.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Frontend: **Vitest** for a few pure utils; **Playwright** e2e for the golden path (import → export CSV → open RSVP link → submit → dashboard → seat → auto-seat → export) in he+RTL and en+LTR, including a drag test. No presentational snapshot tests.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "CI: GitHub Actions (lint, types, tests, openapi-types drift check, docker build).", "ordered": false}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "29-license", "title": "29. License", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "**Rec: AGPL-3.0** if you want improvements by hosted forks to stay open (prevents closed SaaS clones, matches \"no SaaS lock-in\" spirit); **MIT/Apache-2.0** if you want max adoption/contribution and don't mind a company hosting it closed. Apache-2.0 adds patent grant. Neither affects self-hosters. My pick: **AGPL-3.0**, but it's your call (below). No license file is added until you choose."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "30-roadmap-follows-your-phases-each-ends-demoable-with-seed", "title": "30. Roadmap (follows your phases; each ends demoable with seed data)", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Repo, compose, FastAPI skeleton, Alembic, auth/setup/owner-admin, i18n+RTL shell, events CRUD.", "ordered": true, "n": 1}, {"text": "Invitation/member/contact/group CRUD, phone lib, import pipeline + mapping UI + preview/conflicts, filters/search/export.", "ordered": true, "n": 2}, {"text": "Tokens, public RSVP page, shared `set_rsvp`, admin quick-edit, dashboard.", "ordered": true, "n": 3}, {"text": "Messaging Protocol, templates, preview, TestCsv export, message states.", "ordered": true, "n": 4}, {"text": "Seating canvas (tables CRUD, drag, panel, manual seat, locks, warnings, snapshots).", "ordered": true, "n": 5}, {"text": "OR-Tools solver, weights, modes, constraints UI, explanations.", "ordered": true, "n": 6}, {"text": "Exports (by table, alphabetical, print CSS), polish, docs, e2e tests, seed data. Future: WhatsApp Cloud provider + webhook + worker.", "ordered": true, "n": 7}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "decisions-confirmed-by-user", "title": "Decisions confirmed by user", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "License **MIT** (add LICENSE file in phase 1) · manual overflow **allowed** with warning · Maybe = per-invitation opt-in · default region IL, Gregorian dates · Caddy for serving (default, unchallenged)."}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "simplifications-im-deliberately-making-call-out-if-you-disag", "title": "Simplifications I'm deliberately making (call out if you disagree)", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": [{"text": "Server-side sessions table rather than JWT; no refresh logic.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "In-memory rate limiter; no Redis/Celery until WhatsApp bulk sending.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Invitation = seating party; no splitting.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Snapshots instead of undo/redo.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "Plain (non-hashed) RSVP tokens.", "ordered": false}, {"text": "No separate import-staging tables beyond a TTL'd parsed-sheet blob.", "ordered": false}]}], "subsections": []}, {"id": "verification-when-implemented", "title": "Verification (when implemented)", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "`docker compose up` from a clean clone → open `/` → setup owner → load demo seed → run e2e golden path; `pytest` and `playwright test` green; `docker compose down && up` retains data."}], "subsections": []}], "tasks": [], "callouts": [{"kind": "note", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "CHECK `attendee_count >= 0`; partial index on `phone_e164`; `seat_assignment.invitation_id` unique ⇒ a party sits at one table. Snapshots store `{tables:[{id,x,y,...}], assignments:[{inv,table,locked}]}` as JSONB (small, restore = transactional replace). `ON DELETE CASCADE` from event ⇒ deleting an event/invitation really deletes personal data."}], "at": "2-database-schema-postgresql-alembic", "section": "2-database-schema-postgresql-alembic"}], "diagrams": [], "files": [{"path": ".env", "at": ["27-security-privacy"]}, {"path": ".env.example", "at": ["26-docker-self-hosting"]}, {"path": ".gitignore", "at": ["27-security-privacy"]}, {"path": "PRIVACY.md", "at": ["27-security-privacy"]}, {"path": "app/", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "app/core/", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "docker-compose.dev.yml", "at": ["26-docker-self-hosting"]}, {"path": "en.json", "at": ["25-rtl-i18n"]}, {"path": "features/", "at": ["15-react-structure"]}, {"path": "he.json", "at": ["25-rtl-i18n"]}, {"path": "imports/headers.py", "at": ["9-column-mapping"]}, {"path": "messaging/service.py", "at": ["11-messaging-provider-architecture"]}, {"path": "models.py", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "router.py", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "schemas.py", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "seating/config.py", "at": ["2123-automatic-seating"]}, {"path": "seating/conflicts.py", "at": ["2123-automatic-seating"]}, {"path": "service.py", "at": ["16-rest-api-structure-api"]}, {"path": "src/", "at": ["15-react-structure"]}], "roadmap": {"ids": ["1-domain-model", "2-database-schema-postgresql-alembic", "3-authentication", "4-event-model", "5-invitation-member-contact", "6-rsvp-state-model", "7-secure-rsvp-tokens", "8-import-architecture", "9-column-mapping", "10-phone-normalization", "11-messaging-provider-architecture", "12-testcsvmessagingprovider", "13-preparedmessage", "14-future-whatsappcloudmessagingprovider", "15-react-structure", "16-rest-api-structure-api", "20-seating-party", "24-snapshots", "25-rtl-i18n", "26-docker-self-hosting", "27-security-privacy", "28-testing", "29-license", "30-roadmap-follows-your-phases-each-ends-demoable-with-seed"], "basis": "titles"}, "stats": {"words": 2935, "code_blocks": 4}, "source": "# Open-source wedding/event RSVP + seating system — Architecture Proposal\n\n## Context\nGreenfield repo (`/Users/galhavshush/Projects/rsvp-system`, empty). Self-hosted modular monolith: FastAPI + PostgreSQL + React/Vite/TS/Tailwind, Hebrew-first RTL, WhatsApp deferred behind a provider interface (CSV test mode). This document answers the 30 requested points; each decision = **Rec / Why / Alt / Tradeoff**. Nothing is implemented until you approve.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Domain model\nAggregates: **Event** → owns **Group**, **Invitation** (→ **Member**, **Contact**), **Table**, **Snapshot**, **SeatingConstraint**, **MessageTemplate**, **MessageBatch**. **User** is global (admins). Everything event-scoped carries `event_id`.\n- Invitation = RSVP unit **and** default Seating Party (`seats_needed = attendee_count` when status=coming).\n- Seating assignment is a row linking Invitation → Table; layout lives on Table. Never mixed.\n\n## 2. Database schema (PostgreSQL, Alembic)\n```\nuser(id, email unique, password_hash, role[owner|admin], created_at, disabled)\nevent(id, name, type, date, time, venue, address, waze_url, maps_url, hosts, rsvp_deadline,\n      default_language, notes, created_at)\nguest_group(id, event_id, name, color; unique(event_id,name))\ninvitation(id, event_id, display_name, group_id?, rsvp_token unique, rsvp_status[no_response|coming|not_coming|maybe],\n      attendee_count int?, rsvp_updated_at?, rsvp_source[guest_web|admin_manual|whatsapp]?, rsvp_note, notes,\n      include_maybe_in_seating bool default false, message_state[not_prepared|prepared|exported])\ninvitation_member(id, invitation_id, name, position)\ninvitation_contact(id, invitation_id, member_id?, phone_raw, phone_e164?, phone_valid, position)\nseating_table(id, event_id, name, capacity, x, y, shape[round|rect], width, height, zone?, notes,\n      position_locked, assignments_locked)\nseat_assignment(invitation_id PK/unique, table_id, locked bool)      -- one row per seated invitation\nseating_constraint(id, event_id, type[together|apart], invitation_a, invitation_b)\nseating_snapshot(id, event_id, label, kind[before_auto|auto|manual|final], created_at, data jsonb)\nmessage_template(id, event_id, name, body, language)\nmessage_batch(id, event_id, provider, created_at, recipient_count)   -- audit of exports\n```\nNotes: CHECK `attendee_count >= 0`; partial index on `phone_e164`; `seat_assignment.invitation_id` unique ⇒ a party sits at one table. Snapshots store `{tables:[{id,x,y,...}], assignments:[{inv,table,locked}]}` as JSONB (small, restore = transactional replace). `ON DELETE CASCADE` from event ⇒ deleting an event/invitation really deletes personal data.\n\n## 3. Authentication\n**Rec:** server-side sessions in a `session` table (random 256-bit id, hash stored), `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` cookie; argon2id via `argon2-cffi`; CSRF via double-submit header for mutating requests (SameSite=Lax + custom `X-Requested-With` check is sufficient for same-origin deployment). `/api/auth/setup` works only while `user` table is empty → creates Owner. Owner creates Admins. FastAPI dependencies `current_admin`, `require_owner`.\n**Alt:** JWT in cookie (can't revoke, no benefit); fastapi-users (heavy, pulls in OAuth/etc. we explicitly don't want).\n**Tradeoff:** one extra DB lookup per request — irrelevant at this scale. Login rate limit.\n\n## 4. Event model\nStraight table above; `type` is a free string/enum (`wedding` default) — no wedding-only columns. Single active event in UI via an event switcher (data model supports many).\n\n## 5. Invitation / member / contact\nNormalized as specified. Contact optionally links to a member. Display name independent of members. Spreadsheet's `person_N/phone_N` exist only in import/export adapters.\n\n## 6. RSVP state model\nSingle set of columns on `invitation`. One service function `rsvp_service.set_rsvp(invitation, status, count, note, source)` used by **both** public endpoint and admin endpoint ⇒ one system. Rules: `coming` ⇒ count ≥ 1 (default = member count or 1); other statuses ⇒ count null (or 0 for not_coming). Sets `rsvp_updated_at`, `rsvp_source`. No history table (MVP). Changing status away from `coming` doesn't delete the seat assignment automatically; it raises a seating warning (\"seated but not coming\") — admin decides.\n\n## 7. Secure RSVP tokens\n`secrets.token_urlsafe(24)` (192 bits) stored in `invitation.rsvp_token` (unique index). Plain storage is acceptable: it is an unguessable bearer link that admins must re-display/export; hashing would prevent re-generating CSVs. Admin can \"regenerate token\" (invalidates old link). Public endpoints: `GET/PUT /api/public/rsvp/{token}`; return only display name, event name/date/venue/links, current RSVP, member first names optional (off by default). Rate limit by IP (slowapi, in-memory; Redis only if multi-worker later) + uniform 404 for bad tokens. `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`, `noindex`, no third-party scripts.\n\n## 8. Import architecture\nPipeline of pure functions, server-side, stateless-in-DB until commit:\n`upload → parse (openpyxl / csv, size+row caps, no macros/formulas evaluated) → ParsedSheet(headers, rows) → detect_mapping → apply mapping → normalize (phones, trim) → validate + dedupe → ImportPreview → commit`.\n- Upload returns `import_id`; parsed sheet kept in a temp row/file (TTL) so mapping can be re-applied without re-upload. Preview endpoint takes `{import_id, mapping, conflict_actions}`. Commit is one transaction.\n- Output of normalization: `list[InvitationDraft(name, members[], contacts[], group)]` — the same DTO the manual create endpoint uses.\n**Alt:** do everything client-side (SheetJS) — duplicates logic in TS, can't detect DB duplicates. Rejected.\n\n## 9. Column mapping\nMapping = `{column_index → target}`, target ∈ `invitation_name | group | person[n] | phone[n] | note | ignore`. Detector: normalized header (lowercase, strip, collapse spaces) matched against synonym dictionary (he/en) plus regex `^(שם|person|name)\\s*(\\d+)$` / `^(טלפון|phone|mobile|נייד)\\s*(\\d+)$`. Unmapped → \"ignore\", user can fix in UI. Person N / Phone N pair by N. Dictionary in one data file (`imports/headers.py`) so new languages = data change.\n\n## 10. Phone normalization\n`phonenumbers` (Python port of libphonenumber). `normalize(raw, default_region=settings.DEFAULT_REGION=\"IL\")` → E.164 or invalid. Handles `050-…`, `972…`, `+972…`. Default region is an env/event setting ⇒ not Israel-only. Original kept in `phone_raw`. Dup detection by `phone_e164`.\n\n## 11. Messaging-provider architecture\n```python\nclass MessagingProvider(Protocol):\n    def prepare_message(self, inv, contact, template, ctx) -> PreparedMessage\n    def send_message(self, msgs: list[PreparedMessage]) -> SendResult   # test: produces CSV artifact\n    def process_webhook(self, headers, body) -> list[StatusUpdate]      # test: NotImplemented\n    def get_delivery_status(self, msg_id) -> Status\n```\nRendering (`render_template`, `build_prepared_messages`) is **provider-independent** and lives in `messaging/service.py`; providers only transport. Registry picks provider by `MESSAGING_PROVIDER=test_csv`.\nHonest note: `prepare_message` is better as shared core logic than a provider method; I keep it on the interface only as a hook (WhatsApp needs template-parameter mapping). Rec: **`render` in core; provider gets `to_transport_payload(PreparedMessage)`**. Shipping with just the 4-method Protocol you listed minus anything unused would be simplest — flag if you want exactly your 4.\n\n## 12. TestCsvMessagingProvider\n`send_message(msgs)` writes UTF-8-**with-BOM** CSV (so Excel renders Hebrew) columns: Invitation Name, Recipient Name, Phone Number, RSVP Link, Message. Returns bytes streamed as download; records `message_batch`; sets invitation `message_state=exported` (never \"sent\"). Per-recipient exclusion is request-level (list of contact ids), not stored.\n\n## 13. PreparedMessage\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass PreparedMessage:\n    invitation_id; invitation_name; recipient_name; contact_id\n    phone_number (raw); normalized_phone_number; rsvp_url; rendered_message; language\n```\nTemplate variables: `{recipient_name} {invitation_name} {rsvp_url} {event_name} {event_date} {venue}`; rendered with a restricted `str.format_map`-style substitution (no Jinja ⇒ no template injection). Contacts with invalid phone are listed as \"excluded: invalid phone\" in preview.\n\n## 14. Future WhatsAppCloudMessagingProvider\nSame Protocol. Adds: template-name + param mapping, HTTP client (httpx) to Graph API, `POST /api/webhooks/whatsapp` (GET verify challenge + `X-Hub-Signature-256` HMAC check with app secret), `message_delivery(message_id, contact_id, status, ts)` table, retry via a small worker (arq/RQ) — **added only then**. Interactive reply buttons → `rsvp_service.set_rsvp(source='whatsapp')`. Inbound phone → invitation via `phone_e164` match. Zero changes to RSVP/invitation code.\n\n## 15. React structure\nVite + React 18 + TS + React Router + TanStack Query + Tailwind, as you listed:\n`src/{features/{auth,events,invitations,import,rsvp,messaging,seating},components,api,hooks,i18n,layouts,types,utils}`. Two route trees/layouts: `AdminLayout` (auth-guarded) and `GuestLayout` (tiny, lazy-loaded chunk so RSVP page stays fast). Local UI state via `useState/useReducer`; seating canvas local state (drag in progress) separate from server state with optimistic mutations.\nUI kit: Tailwind + **Radix UI primitives** (dialogs, dropdowns, popovers; direction-aware) with shadcn/ui-style copy-in components. Table: TanStack Table.\n\n## 16. REST API structure (`/api`)\n```\nauth:        POST setup | login | logout ; GET me ; POST/GET users (owner)\nevents:      CRUD /events\ngroups:      CRUD /events/{e}/groups\ninvitations: CRUD /events/{e}/invitations (+filters/sort/search/pagination) ; PUT /invitations/{id}/rsvp ;\n             POST /invitations/{id}/regenerate-token ; nested members/contacts CRUD ; GET /events/{e}/invitations/export\nimports:     POST /events/{e}/imports (upload) ; POST /imports/{id}/preview ; POST /imports/{id}/commit\nmessaging:   GET/PUT templates ; POST /events/{e}/messages/preview ; POST /events/{e}/messages/export (CSV)\ndashboard:   GET /events/{e}/stats\ntables:      CRUD /events/{e}/tables ; POST /tables/{id}/duplicate\nseating:     GET /events/{e}/seating (tables+assignments+warnings) ; PUT/DELETE /seating/assignments ;\n             POST /seating/auto ; snapshots: GET/POST/POST restore ; constraints CRUD ; GET export?format=by_table|alpha\npublic:      GET/PUT /public/rsvp/{token}\n```\nBackend layout per domain: `app/<domain>/{router.py, service.py, models.py, schemas.py}` + `app/core/{db,config,security}`; routers thin, logic in services; pure algorithms (phone, mapping, optimizer, capacity) in dependency-free modules → unit-testable without DB.\n\n**Typed client:** generate TS types from FastAPI OpenAPI with **`openapi-typescript` + `openapi-fetch`** (tiny, no codegen runtime). Alt: orval (generates TanStack Query hooks; more magic, bigger output), hey-api. Tradeoff: I hand-write thin query hooks, but types never drift; `npm run gen:api` + CI check that generated file is current.\n\n## 17–19. Seating editor, libraries, table/venue model\n**Rec:** two layers.\n1. **Canvas = SVG (or absolutely-positioned divs in a transformed container) in React**, with pan/zoom via a small transform (`react-zoom-pan-pinch` or hand-rolled ~40 lines). Table dragging via **`@dnd-kit/core`** pointer sensors (touch + mouse + keyboard, no HTML5-DnD flakiness), snapping to optional grid, collision/overlap checked in my own simple AABB function. Canvas uses `direction: ltr` on its container and logical X/Y in px ⇒ never mirrored by RTL.\n2. **Invitation drag** from the Unassigned panel / between tables uses the **same dnd-kit `DndContext`**: tables are droppables, invitation chips are draggables. One library for both ⇒ one mental model. Panel and canvas share a context; auto-scroll handled by dnd-kit.\n- Selection: click/shift-click on tables (simple state); no marquee in MVP.\n- **Alternatives:** *react-konva* (canvas; great perf for hundreds of nodes, but a11y poor, DnD from DOM panel → canvas needs custom hit-testing, text/Hebrew rendering in canvas is meh); *react-flow/xyflow* (graph editor, brings zoom/pan/drag for free but is node/edge-centric and awkward for dropping DOM items onto nodes + licensing/branding nudge); *react-dnd* (HTML5 backend poor on touch); *tldraw* (too heavy, licence restrictions). dnd-kit + SVG is best for ≤ ~60 tables, which is realistic for weddings.\n- RTL: dnd-kit deltas are screen-space, so no mirroring bug; only the *panel/sidebar position* flips with `dir`, canvas stays LTR; `dir=\"auto\"` on user text. Test in both directions.\n\n**Table & layout:** `seating_table` holds layout (x,y,shape,w,h,zone); `seat_assignment` holds assignment. Moving a table updates only x/y. Future items (stage, dance floor, background image) = a future `venue_object` table + `event.floorplan_image`; the canvas just renders more layers. Not built now.\n\n**Persistence:** drag-end `PATCH` of table position (debounced for keyboard nudge), optimistic update.\n\n## 20. Seating Party\nParty = Invitation. `seats_needed = attendee_count` if coming; `maybe` only when `include_maybe_in_seating`; others excluded. `unassigned` panel lists eligible parties not in `seat_assignment`, with RSVP badge, group color, count. Splitting later = new `seating_party` table; the optimizer already works over \"parties\" with `size`, so adding that is localized. Not built now.\n\n## 21–23. Automatic seating\n**Rec: OR-Tools CP-SAT**, deterministic (`random_seed=0`, `num_workers=1`, fixed time limit e.g. 10 s; inputs sorted by id).\n- Vars: `x[p,t]` ∈ {0,1}. Each party → ≤1 table.\n- **Hard:** Σ size·x ≤ capacity − locked_occupancy per table; locked assignments & locked tables fixed (parties on locked tables are removed from the problem, their occupancy is subtracted); `together` pairs equal tables; `apart` pairs not equal; parties never split.\n- **Soft (maximize score, weights in one `SeatingWeights` dataclass in `seating/config.py`, overridable by env):** same-group pairs co-seated (+), group isolation penalty (party alone of its group at a table when group has ≥2 parties), underfill penalty (table occupancy < threshold fraction), unassigned penalty (very large, so seating everyone is preferred but infeasible instances still return a partial result), mild preference to keep parties at their *current* table when mode=reoptimize (stability).\n- Pairwise same-group terms: linearized per (group, table) with `n[g,t]` counts → cheaper than pair vars: reward `min`-style via concave piecewise terms, or simply `y[g,t]` \"group present at table\" minimized (fewer tables per group). I'll use \"minimize number of tables per group\" + isolation — simple and explainable.\n- Modes: `unassigned_only` (all existing assignments fixed) and `reoptimize_unlocked`.\n- Output: assignments + **explanation** (per-party reasons, list of unplaced parties and why) — no black box.\n**Locking:** `seat_assignment.locked` (per assignment) + `table.assignments_locked` (all on that table) + `table.position_locked` (layout only). Invitation-level lock = assignment lock.\n**Capacity over-allocation recommendation:** *Yes, allow manual overflow with a strong red warning (e.g. `11/10`), never automatic.* Why: real venues add chairs; blocking drops would force workarounds. Cost: a soft invariant instead of a DB constraint, so warnings are computed server-side and shown live. Optimizer treats capacity as hard.\n**Conflicts** (computed by `seating/conflicts.py`, pure function, returned with each `GET /seating` and mutation): over_capacity, locked_conflict (move blocked by lock → UI asks to unlock), party_split (n/a until splitting), apart_violation, together_violation, unassigned_confirmed, seated_but_not_coming, maybe_seated.\nAuto Seat endpoint: snapshot \"Before Auto Seat\" → run solver → write assignments → return diff; the editor remains fully interactive.\n\n## 24. Snapshots\nJSONB of layout + assignments (+locks). Auto-created before Auto Seat; manual \"Save snapshot\"; restore = auto-snapshot current (\"Before restore\") then replace tables/assignments in one transaction (restoring tables by id; deleted tables are recreated). Undo/redo: deferred — snapshots cover the need.\n\n## 25. RTL / i18n\n**react-i18next** + JSON namespaces `he.json`/`en.json` (Hebrew default, lazy-loaded), ESLint `i18next/no-literal-string` to enforce zero hardcoded strings. `<html lang dir>` updated on language change; Tailwind **logical utilities** (`ms-*`, `ps-*`, `text-start`, `start-0`) — never `ml/left`. Radix `DirectionProvider`. Numbers/dates via `Intl` with locale (Hebrew locale; Gregorian default, optional Hebrew calendar later). Bidi: `dir=\"auto\"` or `<bdi>` for names/phones (phones forced `dir=\"ltr\"`). Icons that imply direction (chevrons, back) flip; seating canvas explicitly `dir=ltr`. Font: Heebo/Assistant (self-hosted via fontsource — no Google CDN, privacy). Backend messages are error *codes* translated client-side; message templates carry a `language`. Playwright tests run in both languages.\n\n## 26. Docker / self-hosting\n`docker compose up`: services `db` (postgres:16, named volume `pgdata`), `api` (python:3.12-slim, runs `alembic upgrade head` on start, uvicorn), `web` (multi-stage build → static files served by **Caddy**, which also reverse-proxies `/api` to `api` → same origin, so cookies/CSRF trivial; automatic HTTPS in prod via `DOMAIN` env). Dev override: `docker-compose.dev.yml` with Vite HMR + uvicorn `--reload`. `.env.example`; `SECRET_KEY` generated-instructions; `make seed` / `python -m app.seed` for demo. Docs: backup `pg_dump`, restore `psql`, upgrade steps.\n**Alt:** nginx (more config), FastAPI serving static (couples build, fine but less standard). One worker process initially ⇒ in-memory rate limiter acceptable (documented ceiling).\n\n## 27. Security / privacy\nArgon2id, session table + revocation, CSRF header, secure cookies, login throttling, CORS closed (same-origin), Pydantic validation everywhere, upload cap (e.g. 5 MB / 5k rows), openpyxl `read_only`, `data_only=True`, never execute formulas; CSV injection defense on export (prefix `'` to cells starting `= + - @`; keeps Excel-safe). Tokens 192-bit; public API minimal; security headers via Caddy; secrets only via env; `.gitignore` for `.env`; logs omit phones/tokens. Delete event/invitation = hard delete cascade. `PRIVACY.md`: what goes to Meta later (recipient phone, rendered message text/template params, RSVP link; inbound message content/phone from webhook) and that nothing leaves the server today; no analytics/fonts/CDNs on RSVP page. Webhook later: HMAC verification, verify-token handshake.\n\n## 28. Testing\n- Backend **pytest** + real Postgres (testcontainers or compose service): auth/session, phone normalization (table-driven), header detection, import pipeline + duplicates, invitation/member/contact create, token entropy/uniqueness & 404 behavior, RSVP update (public + admin share same state), message rendering, CSV output (BOM, one row per contact, CSV-injection), capacity logic, locks, optimizer constraints (hard constraints hold, locked untouched, deterministic: same input ⇒ same output, group clustering), snapshot restore round-trip.\n- Frontend: **Vitest** for a few pure utils; **Playwright** e2e for the golden path (import → export CSV → open RSVP link → submit → dashboard → seat → auto-seat → export) in he+RTL and en+LTR, including a drag test. No presentational snapshot tests.\n- CI: GitHub Actions (lint, types, tests, openapi-types drift check, docker build).\n\n## 29. License\n**Rec: AGPL-3.0** if you want improvements by hosted forks to stay open (prevents closed SaaS clones, matches \"no SaaS lock-in\" spirit); **MIT/Apache-2.0** if you want max adoption/contribution and don't mind a company hosting it closed. Apache-2.0 adds patent grant. Neither affects self-hosters. My pick: **AGPL-3.0**, but it's your call (below). No license file is added until you choose.\n\n## 30. Roadmap (follows your phases; each ends demoable with seed data)\n1. Repo, compose, FastAPI skeleton, Alembic, auth/setup/owner-admin, i18n+RTL shell, events CRUD.\n2. Invitation/member/contact/group CRUD, phone lib, import pipeline + mapping UI + preview/conflicts, filters/search/export.\n3. Tokens, public RSVP page, shared `set_rsvp`, admin quick-edit, dashboard.\n4. Messaging Protocol, templates, preview, TestCsv export, message states.\n5. Seating canvas (tables CRUD, drag, panel, manual seat, locks, warnings, snapshots).\n6. OR-Tools solver, weights, modes, constraints UI, explanations.\n7. Exports (by table, alphabetical, print CSS), polish, docs, e2e tests, seed data.\nFuture: WhatsApp Cloud provider + webhook + worker.\n\n## Decisions confirmed by user\nLicense **MIT** (add LICENSE file in phase 1) · manual overflow **allowed** with warning · Maybe = per-invitation opt-in · default region IL, Gregorian dates · Caddy for serving (default, unchallenged).\n\n## Simplifications I'm deliberately making (call out if you disagree)\n- Server-side sessions table rather than JWT; no refresh logic.\n- In-memory rate limiter; no Redis/Celery until WhatsApp bulk sending.\n- Invitation = seating party; no splitting.\n- Snapshots instead of undo/redo.\n- Plain (non-hashed) RSVP tokens.\n- No separate import-staging tables beyond a TTL'd parsed-sheet blob.\n\n## Verification (when implemented)\n`docker compose up` from a clean clone → open `/` → setup owner → load demo seed → run e2e golden path; `pytest` and `playwright test` green; `docker compose down && up` retains data.\n", "base": "../../../.claude/plans"}, "config": {"views": ["overview", "diagrams", "steps", "files", "document"], "accent": "#3f9d68", "tldr": ["Self-hosted modular monolith: FastAPI, PostgreSQL, React/Vite/TS/Tailwind, Hebrew-first RTL.", "An invitation is both the RSVP unit and the seating party; one set_rsvp function serves guests and admins.", "WhatsApp is deferred behind a messaging provider interface; a test provider exports CSV.", "Seating uses a dnd-kit canvas, an OR-Tools solver, locks and snapshots.", "Seven roadmap phases, each demoable with seed data."], "diagrams": [{"title": "Serving architecture", "description": "Components named in the plan and how requests flow between them.", "mermaid": "flowchart TD\n  W[React app] --> C[Caddy]\n  C -->|/api proxy| A[FastAPI]\n  A --> D[(PostgreSQL)]\n  A --> M[Messaging provider]", "sources": ["26-docker-self-hosting", "11-messaging-provider-architecture"], "inferred": true}]}};
